WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.490
Two fair coins are tossed independently.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.370
You know that at least one of them shows heads.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.070
What is the probability that both show heads?

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.870
Name the coins first and second.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.230
There are four equally likely outcomes: head head,

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.290
head tail, tail head, tail tail.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.760
"At least one head" rules out tail tail.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.520
Three outcomes survive: head head,

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:27.280
head tail and tail head.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:29.240
They are still equally likely.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.590
Only one of the three survivors has two heads.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.110
The probability is one out of three.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.300
Compare a slightly different piece of information.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.240
Suppose you are told the first coin shows heads.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:44.460
Now only head head and head tail survive,

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.460
and the probability of two heads is one half.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.140
The exact wording of the information matters.

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.490
Information removes outcomes, and the survivors become

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:55.400
the new total.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:59.730
Rebuild this example, then try: two coins are tossed and

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.880
you are told they do not show the same face.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.720
What is the probability the first coin is heads?
